		<content type="html" xml:base="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/viewtopic.php?p=4147#p4147"><![CDATA[
What is the advantage of a three axis SAP over simply testing only one axis and then directly test the two remaining axes of the potential pair. The two AABBs are at that moment already in the cache so the test should be relatively fast. I looked at the implementation in Bullet where potential pairs are added and removed whenever a potential pair is identified as either colliding or separating. That  moves way to many memory in my opinion.  <br><br>I see that this could in the worst case become an O(n^2) test, right? But you could choose any arbitrary axis which fits your game. Any experiences with this?<br><br>Anyway what other strategies exist to keep track of the potential pairs instead of constantly adding and removing them into a list? Ideas?<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=14">Dirk Gregorius</a> — Mon May 07, 2007 1:10 pm</p><hr />
]]></content>
